= Medjool Dates

_Scope: This entry treats the_ date palm fruit _—_ Phoenix dactylifera _L. (Arecaceae) — the_ date _of Levantine, North African, Iberian, Persian, and South Asian cuisines, with the_ Medjool _cultivar as the specific named cultivar most common in modern Western commerce. The_ jujube date_ (Ziziphus jujuba, Rhamnaceae — see_ jujube-dates.typ_) is a_ different plant _despite the shared English word "date." Status: this is one of the most canonical of all Tibb fruits, with rich Qurʾānic and Prophetic-medicine attestation (Sūrat Maryam 19:25; multiple hadith); also classical in Ayurveda (_kharjūra_), Greco-Roman (_dactylus / phoinix_), and modern nutrition / pharmacology. It is_ not _classical in Chinese medicine (the date palm is a Mediterranean / Persian / Levantine tree, outside the Bencao range); modern Chinese 椰棗 (yēzǎo) is a 20th-century coinage. The_ named-stage _profile (busr / rutab / tamr — the Arabic four-stage maturation system) is taken as the canonical framework for the modern reading._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Phoenix dactylifera_ L., family Arecaceae (Palmae). The principal drug-and-food is the *fruit* — a single-seeded drupe — at any of its four canonical maturation stages: *kimrī* (unripe green), *khalāl / busr* (ripe but still firm, yellow or red), *ruṭab* (soft, ripe, fresh — the just-picked register), and *tamr* (fully cured / partly dried — the storage register). Latin *Phoenix* from Greek φοῖνιξ (_phoinix_) — a multivalent Greek word denoting at once the mythical bird, the Phoenicians, the palm tree, and the purple-red dye, with the palm sense almost certainly the "Phoenician [tree]," reflecting the early-Greek import of dates from the Levant. The species epithet *dactylifera* is "finger-bearing," from Latin *dactylus* < Greek δάκτυλος (_dáktylos_, "finger") — for the finger-shaped fruits.#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _phoenix_, _dactylus_); Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_ (s.v. φοῖνιξ). The Phoenician / palm semantic complex: standard classical lexicography; the multivalent φοῖνιξ is one of the great underdetermined Greek words.]

*English:* _Date_, from Old French _date_, from Late Latin _dactylus_; the word entered English in the 13th century. *Medjool* (or *Medjoul* in earlier transliterations) — the *cultivar* name, from Arabic مجدول (_majdūl_, "twisted, braided," referring to the leaf or bunch arrangement) or, on a competing etymology, مجهول (_majhūl_, "unknown" — for the cultivar's originally obscure Moroccan provenance). The Medjool cultivar was identified and saved in the Tafilalt oasis (southeastern Morocco) and transferred to California in the 1920s by the USDA after Moroccan groves were devastated by Bayoud disease; the modern Israeli, Californian, Saudi, and Egyptian Medjool industries descend from these salvaged stocks. There are *hundreds of named date cultivars* (_Deglet Noor_, _Khadrawy_, _Halawi_, _Barhi_, _Zahidi_, _Ajwa_, _Anbara_, _Sukkari_, and many more); Medjool is one of the largest-fruited and richest, prized in modern Western markets.#footnote[Medjool etymology: standard cultivar literature; the _majdūl_ / _majhūl_ controversy is genuine and unsettled. Bayoud / Tafilalt / USDA: standard cultivar-history literature; the Medjool rescue is one of the recognized agricultural-conservation stories of the 20th century.]

*Arabic — the canonical four-stage maturation:* the Arabic tradition gives the date *four distinct names by maturation stage*, each with its own dietetic profile:
- *كِمري (kimrī)* — unripe, green, hard. Not eaten.
- *خَلال (khalāl)* / *بُسر (busr)* — ripe but firm, yellow or red. Astringent-sweet, crisp; eaten fresh in season in the Gulf.
- *رُطَب (ruṭab)* — soft, ripe, fresh; the just-picked register. Sweet, moist, highly perishable. *The date of Sūrat Maryam 19:25 is the ruṭab* (and not the cured tamr).
- *تَمر (tamr)* — fully cured / partly dried; the storage register. Sweet, concentrated, traveling form. *The canonical "date" of the Prophet's diet and of most hadith references is the tamr*.

The classical Tibb mizāj differs by stage — _ruṭab_ and _tamr_ have related but distinct Avicennan placements. Persian: خرما (_khormā_) — the general term, with specific stage-names. Urdu: کھجور (_khajūr_).

*Sanskrit:* *खर्जूर (kharjūra)* — the classical Indian name for the date palm fruit. Charaka, Suśruta, Bhāvaprakāśa. Other forms: *खर्जूरी (kharjūrī)* — the tree; *पिण्डखर्जूर (piṇḍakharjūra)* — the "lump-date," presumably the cured pressed form. The classical Indian context historically encompassed two species: the *Indian wild date* (_Phoenix sylvestris_, the silvery date palm, native to South Asia) and the *imported Mediterranean date* (_P. dactylifera_); the classical _kharjūra_ reading applies broadly to both, with the Mediterranean species treated as the superior nutritive variety. The Hindi term *खजूर (khajūr)* descends from Sanskrit _kharjūra_; *खर्जूर* (Indic medical Sanskrit usage) is preserved in modern Ayurvedic pharmacy.#footnote[Monier-Williams (s.v. _kharjūra_); Charaka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 — _kharjūra_ in the _phala-varga_; Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Āmrādi-phala-varga_, _kharjūra_ entry; P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II.]

*Chinese:* The Mediterranean / Persian date palm is *not classical in Chinese medicine* — the tree is outside the Bencao range. Modern Chinese: *椰棗 (yēzǎo)*, "coconut date" (椰 yē = coconut, by association with the palm); *海棗 (hǎizǎo)*, "sea date"; *波斯棗 (bōsīzǎo)*, "Persian date." All are 20th-century coinages or late-classical exotic-item names. The Chinese tradition uses its own *Ziziphus jujuba* (大棗 dàzǎo) as the canonical "date" in the materia medica — a different plant entirely (see `jujube-dates.typ`).#footnote[椰棗 / 海棗 / 波斯棗 as modern Chinese names: standard Chinese botanical and culinary literature; the date palm is essentially absent from the canonical Bencao Gangmu / Shennong Bencao Jing tradition. Modern Chinese halal cuisine (esp. Hui and Uyghur traditions, with strong Persian / Central Asian influence) does use date palm fruit, but as a regional borrowing, not a Bencao drug.]

*Hebrew:* תָּמָר (_tāmār_) — the canonical biblical name, cognate with Arabic _tamr_; both descend from a Northwest Semitic root for "date / palm." The Hebrew name passes into the personal name Tamar (multiple biblical women); the palm tree is itself a recurring biblical symbol of righteousness ("The righteous shall flourish like the palm tree" — Psalm 92:12). The Hebrew Bible references the palm in Solomon's temple decoration, in the description of paradise in some prophetic texts, in the Song of Songs ("This thy stature is like to a palm tree" — Song of Songs 7:7), and in the use of date palm fronds for the festival of Sukkot (לולב _lūlāv_).

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Phoenix dactylifera has no classical Chinese entry._ Modern Chinese dietetics treats the date palm fruit by reasoned extension on classical axes — the standard placement is *warm (溫), sweet (甘), entering Spleen-Stomach (脾胃), with secondary action on Lung (肺) and Kidney (腎)*; actions *補中益氣 (bǔ zhōng yì qì, tonifies middle, augments qi), 養血 (yǎng xuè, nourishes blood — mild), 潤肺 (rùn fèi, moistens Lung), 滋陰 (zī yīn, nourishes yin)*. The placement is consistent with the herb's intense sweetness, concentrated nutritive density, and the cross-system convergent reading as a *bṛṃhaṇa / muqawwī al-badan* tonic fruit.

The reading is *reasoned extension, not canonical 本草* — flagged. The proximate Chinese analogue is 大棗 (Chinese jujube — see `jujube-dates.typ`), which serves the same dietary-tonic / spleen-stomach-supporting role in the canonical Chinese formulary; *Phoenix dactylifera* is sometimes treated in modern dietetic literature as a Mediterranean / Persian counterpart to 大棗, with the same broad function but a different chemical and nutritional density (higher caloric load, much higher sugar concentration, more concentrated potassium and magnesium).

*Cautions:* damp-heat / phlegm-damp accumulation; diabetes (the very high glycemic load); food stagnation; the warming-sweetening register inappropriate in heat-pattern conditions.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Kharjūra_ (खर्जूर) is *classical*, treated by Charaka in the _phala-varga_ of Sūtrasthāna 27 and elsewhere across the saṃhitā literature; the Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu has a substantial _kharjūra_ entry treating it as one of the canonical *jīvanīya* (vitalizing) fruits.#footnote[Kharjūra in Charaka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (_phala-varga_); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Āmrādi-phala-varga_, _kharjūra_ entry; P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II.]

*Rasa (taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet) — predominant; intensely so, especially in the cured _tamr_ register.

*Guṇa (qualities):* Guru (गुरु, heavy), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous), Sāndra (सान्द्र, dense). The cured fruit is the paradigmatic heavy-snigdha-dense sweet — sharing a guṇa-profile with wheat, milk, ghee.

*Vīrya (potency):* Śīta (शीत, cooling) — *the standout feature*. Despite being grown in extreme hot-dry climate, the ripe and fresh date is read as *cooling* in classical Ayurveda — the corrective is precisely the cooling-snigdha register against the hot-dry climate it grows in. The cured tamr is sometimes read as moderately warming by Bhāvaprakāśa, but the dominant classical reading is cooling.

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of kharjūra: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Āmrādi-phala-varga_; the standout śīta vīrya despite hot-arid cultivation is the canonical "climate-corrective" reading — the date functions as a cooling-snigdha corrective for the climate of its growth.]

*Prabhāva:* No distinct prabhāva is assigned in the core saṃhitās. The intensely *jīvanīya* (vitalizing, life-promoting) action and the rapid energy-restoring effect — both well-known properties used in convalescence, in fasting-breaking, and in postpartum care — are treated as flowing from the madhura-guru-snigdha-śīta-madhura-vipāka profile rather than as a separate prabhāva. Some modern commentators read the rapid blood-sugar-stabilizing effect (relevant to the Prophetic practice of breaking the fast with dates and water) as a prabhāva-level action; this is interpretive — flagged.

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies *Vāta and Pitta* (vāta-pitta-śāmaka) — strongly, on the snigdha-madhura-śīta profile, in the canonical anabolic-Vāta-Pitta-pacifying register; increases *Kapha* (sthaulya / obesity caveat) at heavy daily dose. The vāta-pitta-pacifying register is the principal classical Ayurvedic clinical use.

*Karma (actions):*
- _Jīvanīya_ — life-promoting; the headline action.
- _Bṛṃhaṇa, Balya, Puṣṭida_ — anabolic / bulk-building / nourishing.
- _Vṛṣya_ — aphrodisiac, reproductive-tissue building.
- _Hṛdya_ — cardiotonic; nourishing to the Heart.
- _Tarpaṇa_ — replenishing / satisfying.
- _Snehana_ — mildly unctuous / lubricating.
- _Bala-pradāyī_ — strength-giving.
- _Śrama-hara_ — relieves fatigue / exhaustion.
- _Used in:_ _kārśya_ (emaciation), _kṣaya_ (wasting), convalescence, postpartum care, _dhātu-kṣaya_ (tissue depletion), _bala-kṣaya_ (strength-depletion), exertion-induced exhaustion, _ārtava-kṣaya_ (low menstrual flow), _vājīkaraṇa_ (aphrodisiac) regimens, _rasāyana_ (rejuvenation) contexts.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S) Conditional viruddha:* heavy daily _kharjūra_ in Kapha excess, _āma_, _sthaulya_ (obesity), _prameha_ (diabetes-class), low _agni_, _madhumeha_ — the standard guru-snigdha-madhura Kapha-aggravation caution. The classical Indian _prameha_ caveat anticipates the modern diabetic concern with remarkable consistency.
- *(N/F) Combination cautions:* dates + milk in heavy quantity may exceed appropriate _snigdha-bala_; dates + curd (_dadhi_) compound the heavy-sweet-snigdha; eating cured tamr in excess at night is folk-cautioned as Kapha-aggravating.
- *(M) Modern medical:*
  - *Diabetes / glycemic load:* dates have a *very high natural sugar concentration* (~65–80 g sugar per 100 g) and a *moderate-high glycemic index*; for diabetic patients, intake must be moderated — though intriguingly, some clinical studies of Medjool and other date varieties show *lower-than-expected* postprandial glycemic response, possibly due to fiber and polyphenols, with the magnitude varying by cultivar. Verify against current diabetology guidelines.
  - *Calorie density:* ~270 kcal / 100 g (cured); easy to overconsume.
  - *Choking hazard for young children:* whole dates with pits.
  - *Sulfite preservation:* some commercial dates are sulfite-treated; sulfite allergy.
  - *Mold / aflatoxin risk:* improperly stored dates can develop mold; sourcing matters.#footnote[Conditional doṣa cautions: standard Ayurvedic principle for guru-snigdha-madhura fruits. (M) glycemic load: dates' relatively-favorable-for-the-sugar-content glycemic response is documented in several clinical studies, esp. on Medjool — but the absolute sugar content remains the limiting factor in diabetic management. Verify against current diabetology / endocrinology guidelines.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Phoenix dactylifera is one of the most canonical of all Tibb fruits and the most heavily Qurʾānically attested food of any in the materia medica._ Avicenna treats it extensively in _al-Qānūn_ Book II; Ibn al-Bayṭār in _al-Jāmiʿ_; Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya devotes substantial sections of _al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī_ to its various stages and uses. The Qurʾānic and Prophetic-medicine register is particularly rich — see below. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ` for the framework.

*Arabic:* the four-stage system — *kimrī* (unripe), *busr / khalāl* (firm-ripe), *ruṭab* (soft fresh), *tamr* (cured) — is canonical and is preserved in classical Tibb diagnosis and prescription. The bare term *تَمر (tamr)* often serves as the general name; نَخل (_nakhl_) or نَخلة (_nakhla_) is the tree. Persian: خرما (_khormā_); various stage-names. Urdu: کھجور (_khajūr_). The cultivar names — *عَجوة (ʿajwa)*, *عَنبَرة (ʿanbara)*, *سُكَّري (sukkarī)*, *برني (barnī)*, *مَجدول (majdūl)* — are also classical, with the *ʿajwa* of Madīna having particular Tibb al-Nabawī register (see below).

*Mizāj (by stage):*
- *Ruṭab (fresh):* *Hot and Moist in the 2nd degree* — the nutritive nourishing register, paralleling the Ayurvedic snigdha-madhura.
- *Tamr (cured):* *Hot and Dry in the 2nd degree* — the storage / concentrated form, more drying.
- *Busr / khalāl:* *Cold and Dry in the 1st degree* — the firm-ripe astringent stage, less heating than the ripe forms.
- The *stage-by-stage mizāj differentiation* is the most refined Avicennan treatment of any fruit in the Qānūn, reflecting the date's central dietary and ritual importance in the Arab-Persian world.

*Afʿāl:*
- *Muqawwī al-badan* — *body-strengthening* / general tonic.
- *Mughadhdhī mulayyin* — nutritive and mildly laxative.
- *Mufattiḥ li-l-suddad* — deobstruent (mild).
- *Muḍādd al-sumūm* (مضاد السموم) — *anti-toxin* / counter-poison. The hadith of the seven _ʿajwa_ dates as protection from poison and magic underwrites this Tibb action category specifically.
- *Mufarriḥ* — mood-elevating; sustains spirit.
- *Munaqqī li-l-tabīʿa* — purgative-cleansing (mild).
- *Mughadhdhī ghidhāʾan kaẓīran* — substantially nutritive.
- *Murakhkhī* — relaxing; particularly the obstetric use (see below).
- *Yuqawwī al-bāh* — supports sexual potency.

*Istemālāt:* general weakness, convalescence, fasting-breaking, malnutrition, post-exertion recovery, postpartum strength, anaemia, dyspepsia (in moderation), constipation, _amrāḍ al-balgham_ (phlegmatic diseases — by counter-balancing); obstetric use (Maryam 19:25 — see below).

*Mawāneʿ / Iḥtirāzāt:* hot-mizāj patients (use moderately); diabetic patients (verify against modern diabetology); excessive use causes _ṣadāʿ_ (headache) and _suddat_ (obstruction) in some sources; not for those with *suddat al-kabid* (hepatic congestion) at high daily intake.

*Muṣliḥ:* almonds (_lawz_); rose-water; vinegar; small amounts of poppy in some classical preparations — to balance the heating-and-heaviness.

*Badal:* fig (_tīn_); raisin (_zabīb_); jujube (_ʿunnāb_) for the chest-cleansing register; honey (_ʿasal_) for the energy register.

*Classical Tibb formulas:*
- *ʿAjwa dates as primary food-medicine* — taken on an empty stomach for various uses, with the hadith register driving the prescription.
- *Maʿjūn-i Tamr* (date electuary) — date-paste preparations in Unani pharmacy as nutritive-restorative; many compound formulations.
- *Sharbat-i Khormā* — date syrup, used in convalescence.
- *Date-and-milk preparations* — _laban bi-tamr_, the classical fasting-breaking and breakfast staple of the Arab world.
- *Tahnīk* preparation — the Prophetic practice of chewing dates softly to mix with saliva and placing a small amount on the palate of a newborn — preserved as a Sunna ritual at birth.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation (the central case):*
- *Sūrat Maryam 19:25* — "And shake toward you the trunk of the palm tree; it will drop upon you ripe, fresh dates (_ruṭaban janiyyan_)" — God's instruction to Maryam during her labor with ʿĪsā. The verse is the canonical scriptural attestation of fresh dates (_ruṭab_) as obstetric food-medicine: ripe-fresh ruṭab is read as climatically and constitutionally apt for labor and delivery — easily digested sugars, potassium, mild relaxant of the smooth muscle (modern correspondences). The verse anchors the Tibb-Nabawī obstetric use of ruṭab to this day; in many Muslim cultures, fresh dates are given to labouring women as direct emulation of the Quranic narrative.
- *Hadith of the seven ʿajwa dates of Madīna:* *"Whoever eats seven ʿajwa dates of Madīna in the morning, neither poison nor magic will harm him that day"* (Bukhārī, _Kitāb al-Aṭʿima_; Muslim, _Kitāb al-Ashriba_). The hadith specifies the *ʿajwa* cultivar of Madīna, in the *morning*, in *seven*-count, on an *empty stomach*. The reading carries through into the Tibb al-Nabawī register as a *daily morning practice* and underwrites the canonical *muḍādd al-sumūm* (anti-toxin) action attribution.
- *Hadith on breaking the fast:* *"The Prophet (ﷺ) used to break his fast with _ruṭab_ before he prayed [Maghrib]; if there were no _ruṭab_, then with dried dates (_tamarāt_); if there were no _tamarāt_, then he would sip water"* (Tirmidhī, Abū Dāwūd). The hadith is the canonical underwriter of the Ramadan practice of breaking the fast with dates and water — preserved continuously across the Muslim world.
- *Tahnīk* — the Prophet performed _tahnīk_ for several newborns, chewing a date and placing the softened paste on the palate of the infant (multiple hadiths — Bukhārī, Muslim). The practice is preserved as a Sunna ritual at birth.
- *Sūrat al-Raḥmān 55:11* — "Therein are fruits and palm trees with sheaths" — paradisaical attestation; *Sūrat al-Anʿām 6:99*; *Sūrat al-Naḥl 16:11* — agricultural attestations; *Sūrat ʿAbasa 80:29* — providence attestation.

The date is, in sum, *the most heavily Qurʾānically attested food of any in the materia medica*, with substantive obstetric, daily-prophylactic, fasting-breaking, and newborn-ritual register — a depth of scriptural integration that no other food approaches.#footnote[Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II — _tamr_, _ruṭab_, _busr_ entries; Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_, palm and date entries; Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya, _al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī_ — substantial date sections; al-Suyūṭī, _al-Manhaj al-Sawī_. Qurʾān: Sūrat Maryam 19:23–26 (the Maryam labor narrative); Sūrat al-Raḥmān 55:11, Sūrat al-Anʿām 6:99, Sūrat al-Naḥl 16:11, Sūrat ʿAbasa 80:29, Sūrat al-Wāqiʿah 56:29, Sūrat al-Qamar 54:20, Sūrat Qāf 50:10. Hadith: Bukhārī (_Kitāb al-Aṭʿima_, _Kitāb al-Ṭibb_ — the ʿajwa hadith); Muslim; Tirmidhī, Abū Dāwūd (the breaking-the-fast hadith). _Tahnīk_ hadiths: multiple narrations in Bukhārī, Muslim. The depth of Qurʾānic-hadith integration of the date palm is, for this materia medica, the most extensively-attested of any food.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

*No classical Chinese formulas use Phoenix dactylifera.* The Chinese tradition uses its own 大棗 (Ziziphus jujuba — see `jujube-dates.typ`) as the canonical "date" in formulary practice. Modern Chinese halal cuisine and modern dietetic literature includes 椰棗 (yēzǎo) as an imported nutritive fruit, but this is post-classical and not formulary.

=== Ayurveda

- *Kharjūra-pāka* — date paste / date-cake preparations; classical _bṛṃhaṇa_ and _vājīkaraṇa_ formulations.
- *Kharjūra-mantha* — date-and-water-and-honey draught; nutritive convalescent register.
- *Kharjūra-kṣīrapāka* — dates cooked in milk; the canonical Ayurvedic strengthening preparation, particularly for convalescence, postpartum, and _vājīkaraṇa_ regimens. Date-milk is the Indian counterpart to the Arabic _laban bi-tamr_.
- *Drākṣādi cūrṇa* and related compound powders — dates as supporting nutritive ingredient; many formulas in the rasāyana and bṛṃhaṇa classes.
- *Postpartum and lactation regimens* — dates as one of the *sūtikā-anna* (postpartum foods) across regional Indian traditions; documented in Charaka Sūtrasthāna's _sūtikā-paricaryā_ register.
- *Modern Ayurvedic preparations:* *Khajūr pāk* (a traditional Indian sweet-medicine of dates with ghee, dry fruits, and spices), *Khajūr cūrṇa*, _Mukkāmla khajūr ksheerapakam_ (Kerala preparation).#footnote[Kharjūra formulary preparations: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Āmrādi-phala-varga_; Sharangadhara Saṃhitā; the modern _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_; regional Ayurvedic literature.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Greco-Roman:* dates entered the Roman world via Phoenician and Egyptian trade and were a luxury food in Italy. Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ I.148 treats _phoinix_ as nutritive and demulcent. Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XIII has extensive cultivar descriptions and economic data on the date trade. Galen treats dates as warming and nutritive but laxative-tending. The Romans imported dates principally from Egypt, Palestine, and Mesopotamia; dates appear in Roman cookery (Apicius) as both a culinary and medicinal ingredient.
- *Medieval European:* dates were imported via the Levantine and North African trade through Venice, Genoa, and Marseille; they appear as a luxury sweet and as a medicinal nutritive in monastic-pharmacy electuaries.
- *Modern Mediterranean and global cultivation:* dates remain central in the Maghrebi, Levantine, and Persian cuisines; modern industrial production in Israel, California, Saudi Arabia, Iran, Egypt, Algeria, and Tunisia.
- *Modern Western herbal:* dates as natural sweetener in raw-food and paleo / Whole30 culinary movements; modern phytotherapy uses date extract preparations as a nutritive rather than as a primary herbal medicine.#footnote[Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ I.148 (_phoinix_) — Beck translation; Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XIII (Loeb / Rackham); Galen on dates. Apicius, _De Re Coquinaria_; Renaissance and medieval European date-trade history: standard culinary and trade-history literature.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

Covered above. The Tibb formulary use of dates is *unmatched* in any other tradition: ʿajwa dates as daily prophylactic, the four-stage system in Avicennan prescription, Maʿjūn-i Tamr electuaries, Sharbat-i Khormā syrup, _laban bi-tamr_ as canonical breakfast and fasting-breaking food, _tahnīk_ ritual at birth, Maryam-labor obstetric register, daily Ramadan iftār practice, and the entire culinary register of Arab, Persian, and South-Asian Muslim cuisines.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_Phoenix dactylifera_ is the *canonical desert oasis tree* — adapted to *hot-arid* climates with summer temperatures above 40 °C, low precipitation, and access to groundwater (the oasis pattern: a deep aquifer + surface palm canopy). Native range and primary cultivation: the Persian Gulf and Mesopotamia (the species' likely center of domestication, ~6000 years ago), the Arabian peninsula, the Levant, Egypt, the Maghreb, and (by colonial/modern introduction) California's Coachella Valley, the Sonoran desert, and Israel. The dietetic role of the date is *exemplarily climatically apt* for hot-arid dietetics — and the classical Ayurvedic śīta-vīrya reading captures this with clean precision: the date is *cooling-snigdha-tonifying* corrective for the very hot-dry climate it grows in. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Hot-arid (Saharan, Arabian, Mesopotamian, Levantine) — the canonical climate

The native and canonical climate. _Jāṅgala_ par excellence; extreme dryness (_rūkṣa_), heat (_uṣṇa_), Pitta-Vāta aggravating; 燥 + 熱; Lung + Heart + Kidney loaded (the kidney depletion from fluid loss).

*Traditional preparation.*
- *Tamr (cured) as winter and traveling food* — concentrated nutrition, long shelf-life, the canonical caravan food and the camel-driver's staple.
- *Ruṭab (fresh) as summer harvest food* — eaten fresh at harvest time (late summer / early autumn), often with milk or buttermilk; the Maryam-labor verse uses ruṭab specifically.
- *Laban bi-tamr (milk + dates)* — the classical Arabian breakfast and fasting-breaking food; cooling-snigdha-strengthening composite preparation; a textbook climatic-constitutional pairing.
- *Date-with-yogurt / labneh* — Levantine cooling combinations; mild Pitta-pacifying.
- *Date-honey-and-tahini sweets (e.g. maʿmūl)* — Levantine festive food; combines date with the cooling snigdha of sesame paste.
- *Ḥalwā-yi tamr (Persian)* — date paste with cardamom, saffron, rose-water; a classical Persian preparation.

*Cares.* The hot-arid climate is *exactly the climate dates are constitutionally suited to* — and the classical Arabic-Persian-Levantine preparations are textbook climatically apt: milk pairings, rose-water and saffron accents, fresh ruṭab in summer, cured tamr in winter and travel. The classical Tibb stage-by-stage mizāj differentiation (busr cold-dry, ruṭab hot-moist, tamr hot-dry) gives unusually fine-grained climatic-prescriptive guidance — busr for *summer / heat-pattern*, ruṭab for *labour / restorative*, tamr for *winter / travel / Ramadan*.

=== Mediterranean (warm-dry summer / cool-wet winter) — secondary

Where dates are imported and grown at the Mediterranean basin's hotter and drier zones (Sicily, southern Spain, North African coast).

*Traditional preparation.* Dates as a luxury sweet, in confectionery (Italian _dattero_), in Maghrebi tagines (date + lamb + almond combinations), in Spanish dulces. The medicinal register is preserved in folk and monastic pharmacy.

*Cares.* The cooling-snigdha register is suited to summer; the warming-cured tamr is suited to winter dishes.

=== Continental seasonal-extreme (modern global) — non-traditional

Modern American Midwest, central Europe, northern China — modern globalized dates eaten across the seasonal cycle.

*Cares.* The classical seasonal-stage register is lost in globalized commerce; Medjool dates available year-round are typically cured tamr-equivalent. The modern Western paleo / raw-food register reads dates as a "natural sweetener," ignoring the classical Tibb fine-grained register; not toxic, but the constitutional reading is flattened.

=== Hot-humid tropical (South India, SE Asia, Caribbean, sub-Saharan) — partly traditional via trade

The historical South Asian and Southeast Asian Muslim trade carried dates across the hot-humid zone; modern Indonesian, Malaysian, Bangladeshi, and South Indian Muslim cuisines include dates as iftār food and as celebratory sweet.

*Cares.* In hot-humid Pitta-Kapha climate, dates are dietetically heavy — moderate use in the cool/dry season is more climatically apt than heavy daily intake. The classical Ayurvedic Kapha-aggravation caution applies most strongly in this climate.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_)

The modern Western health-food register has moved dates into smoothies, "energy balls," ice creams, and chilled raw-food preparations — including in climates where dates are not traditional foods. In cool-damp temperate (Atlantic) climates, frequent date consumption in chilled preparations may not do the climatic-constitutional work of the original hot-arid pairing. The classical register is *paired with warming dairy fat (laban, milk, yogurt) in hot-arid climates*; *paired with rose-water and saffron in Persian Levantine summers*; *eaten cured (tamr) in winter, fresh (ruṭab) in summer*. Recognizing the climate-and-season register is the corrective; the modern Western register is not toxic but no longer constitutional. See Caraka Sū. 26 for the _deśa-viruddha_ framework; see `climates.typ` for the hot-arid climate profile.#footnote[Climate framework: see `climates.typ`. Arabian / Persian / Levantine date traditions: standard regional ethnographies. _Laban bi-tamr_: standard Arab cuisine literature. The classical Tibb stage-by-stage mizāj differentiation: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Approximate composition (Medjool dates, cured, per 100 g):*
- Energy ~277 kcal; Water ~21 g (Medjool is moister than _Deglet Noor_ and most cured varieties)
- Carbohydrate ~75 g (~66 g sugar — predominantly fructose and glucose, with sucrose; ratio varies by cultivar); fiber ~7 g
- Protein ~1.8 g; Fat ~0.15 g
- Minerals: *potassium very high* (~700 mg — among the highest of any common food per 100 g); magnesium ~54 mg; calcium ~64 mg; iron ~0.9 mg; manganese, copper, selenium present
- Vitamin K ~2.7 μg; B-vitamins (thiamin, riboflavin, niacin, B6, pantothenic acid, folate) in modest amounts
- Phenolic compounds: chlorogenic acid, ferulic acid, cinnamic acid; tannins (proanthocyanidins); flavonoids; gallic acid
- Polysaccharides — date pectins and other dietary fibers, with prebiotic effect
- Carotenoids in some cultivars (β-carotene, lutein)
- Glycemic index variable by cultivar: *Medjool moderate (~55), Deglet Noor moderate-low (~40), Khalas low-moderate*; the relatively-favorable glycemic response (relative to pure sugar) is documented in clinical studies and is one of the more interesting modern findings about the fruit#footnote[Phytochemistry and nutrition of dates: standard nutritional databases (USDA FoodData Central, Medjool entry); current reviews in _Journal of Food Science and Technology_, _Critical Reviews in Food Science and Nutrition_; date polyphenol research in _Food Chemistry_; glycemic-index studies on Medjool and other cultivars (e.g. Alkaabi et al., _Nutrition Journal_, 2011). The glycemic-index-by-cultivar variation is significant and worth flagging.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Energy supply and rapid sugar absorption* — the canonical "fasting-break" property has straightforward modern explanation: high fructose-glucose content + fiber for stabilization + potassium for rehydration; the Prophetic practice of breaking the fast with dates and water is nutritionally exact.
- *Cardioprotective potential* — high potassium, modest magnesium, polyphenol antioxidants; modest clinical evidence in cardiovascular health.
- *Oxytocin-like / labor-facilitating effects (the Maryam connection)* — clinical evidence is *emerging and partially supportive*: several modern studies (esp. from Jordan, Iran, Egypt — e.g. Al-Kuran et al., 2011; Razali et al., 2014) report that *daily consumption of 6 dates in the late third trimester* is associated with *higher rates of spontaneous labor onset, reduced need for oxytocin augmentation, and shorter latent phase*. The mechanism is unsettled (possibly via fatty acids, oxytocin-receptor binding, or carbohydrate-mediated effects); the studies remain modest and require replication, but they are striking. The Quranic Maryam verse and the modern obstetric data point in the same direction.
- *Antimicrobial and antifungal* — preclinical activity from date polyphenols; modest clinical relevance.
- *Anti-inflammatory and antioxidant* — substantial preclinical literature; modest clinical translation.
- *Prebiotic / gut-microbiome support* — date polysaccharides have demonstrated prebiotic activity; the long-fasting / fast-breaking pattern may benefit from this register.
- *Tahnīk / newborn glucose stabilization* — modern interest: a small number of pediatric studies have explored whether date-paste tahnīk may help stabilize neonatal glucose in vulnerable infants; the evidence is preliminary but interesting given the Sunna register.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Dietetic register:* 3–7 dates per day (~30–70 g) is the standard culinary intake; no toxicological concern at this level in non-diabetic populations.
- *Prophetic-medicine register:* *7 ʿajwa dates on an empty stomach in the morning* — the hadith register; modern Muslim daily practice.
- *Ramadan iftār register:* breaking the fast with 1–3 dates and water before the meal — the canonical practice.
- *Late-pregnancy register (the Maryam protocol, modern obstetric):* 6 dates per day from ~36 weeks gestational age — per the clinical studies, with obstetrician oversight.
- *Diabetes:* significant moderation — 1–2 dates with a meal that includes protein and fat to blunt glycemic response; *do not eat fasting / alone in diabetic patients*; verify against current diabetology guidance.
- *Children:* whole dates are a choking hazard in young children; date paste or chopped dates are safer.
- *Pregnancy:* generally safe and traditionally encouraged in the last weeks; verify against current obstetric guidance.
- *Storage:* cool, dry, sealed; refrigerate or freeze for long storage to prevent mold and aflatoxin.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges from standard nutritional and obstetric literature; the late-pregnancy date intake studies are an active and growing area of clinical research (Al-Kuran et al., _Journal of Obstetrics and Gynaecology_ 2011; Razali et al., _Journal of Obstetrics and Gynaecology Research_ 2014; subsequent meta-analyses). Verify against current obstetric guidelines. The Tibb-Nabawī seven-dates morning practice is preserved as a Sunna; the modern medical literature on its prophylactic effect is preliminary.]

